[SPEAKER_00]: And I explained then better what I was all about.

1046
01:25:52,501 --> 01:25:54,762
[SPEAKER_00]: He finally was able to listen and hear better.

1047
01:25:55,923 --> 01:26:01,006
[SPEAKER_00]: I got my point across and I asked him if I could see him at, say, 7 o'clock.

1048
01:26:01,046 --> 01:26:01,647
[SPEAKER_00]: It was then about 4.

1049
01:26:02,785 --> 01:26:03,546
[SPEAKER_00]: in the afternoon.

1050
01:26:03,626 --> 01:26:08,229
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, yeah, he could see me and get some stuff out.

1051
01:26:08,629 --> 01:26:10,891
[SPEAKER_00]: He's staying with his son there.

1052
01:26:11,151 --> 01:26:12,793
[SPEAKER_00]: His wife had died some years ago.

1053
01:26:12,813 --> 01:26:20,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And he sold his home in Los Angeles and moved to Odessa, where his son is an executive with a local television station.

1054
01:26:20,138 --> 01:26:23,320
[SPEAKER_00]: And they have a couple of kids and a couple of dogs.

1055
01:26:23,401 --> 01:26:25,402
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're all on vacation, left him with the dogs.

1056
01:26:26,443 --> 01:26:28,364
[SPEAKER_00]: And he says, they're spoiled, those dogs are.

1057
01:26:28,644 --> 01:26:30,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Boy, they were yelling all over the place.

1058
01:26:32,036 --> 01:26:40,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we went in and got a hotel room at the Travel Lodge in Odessa and then had a bite to eat at what they call a steak and eggs place nearby.

1059
01:26:40,242 --> 01:26:56,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we drove out and visited the, saw the Globe Theater, saw the world's largest jackrabbit, a little 12-foot statue there in downtown Odessa, took a picture of that, and saw a few of the sights.

1060
01:26:56,413 --> 01:27:00,016
[SPEAKER_00]: Very wide streets and a rather nice city.

1061
01:27:01,263 --> 01:27:06,067
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I guess we'd done that first after we got cleaned up and I got my notes ready for the interview.

1062
01:27:07,088 --> 01:27:09,410
[SPEAKER_00]: At the motel then, we did the sightseeing.

1063
01:27:09,430 --> 01:27:10,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Then went to Steak and Eggs.

1064
01:27:10,531 --> 01:27:15,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I dropped Rita off and I drove out to Gisela's house and got there about 7 o'clock just on time.

1065
01:27:17,778 --> 01:27:22,642
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it would be a good interview because he had a loud voice and he seemed to be ready to talk.

1066
01:27:22,743 --> 01:27:24,504
[SPEAKER_00]: But as it turned out, he didn't say much.

1067
01:27:24,544 --> 01:27:27,247
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are many gaps, as you can hear, on the tape.

1068
01:27:28,227 --> 01:27:32,428
[SPEAKER_00]: would say something, and I thought he was going to follow up and elaborate and say more.

1069
01:27:33,168 --> 01:27:39,190
[SPEAKER_00]: But he just shut up then, and I had to start something new.

1070
01:27:39,330 --> 01:27:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And it seemed like I was always starting something new.

1071
01:27:43,131 --> 01:27:51,113
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to break in with some, after almost an indecent pause, I'd have to start up on another subject.

1072
01:27:51,313 --> 01:27:53,474
[SPEAKER_00]: It was difficult to do.

1073
01:27:54,074 --> 01:27:57,295
[SPEAKER_00]: He had some pretty good stories, but they were

1074
01:27:57,947 --> 01:28:00,468
[SPEAKER_00]: I almost felt like I was wrenching them out of him.

1075
01:28:00,488 --> 01:28:06,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Occasionally, I'd mention something, and he'd light up and lean forward and tell a story.

1076
01:28:07,050 --> 01:28:16,313
[SPEAKER_00]: I mentioned Bill Guthrie, and he told the story about Miller, Huggins, and Ruth, and he told Guthrie through Ruth, out of the game, I'd take your bat boy with you.

1077
01:28:16,333 --> 01:28:19,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, Huggins got Guthrie for that.

1078
01:28:19,514 --> 01:28:23,755
[SPEAKER_00]: He said he wasn't in the league the next year, although I don't know he ever could do anything like that.

1079
01:28:27,343 --> 01:28:33,067
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a little house, one story, I guess, and not too many rooms.

1080
01:28:33,087 --> 01:28:34,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Rather neat inside.

1081
01:28:34,368 --> 01:28:37,371
[SPEAKER_00]: We sat in the living room, and the door was open.

1082
01:28:37,411 --> 01:28:39,412
[SPEAKER_00]: A wind began to blow about halfway through there.

1083
01:28:39,432 --> 01:28:41,594
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought, you know, blow the whole thing down.

1084
01:28:41,634 --> 01:28:42,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Terrific ale.

1085
01:28:42,554 --> 01:28:47,778
[SPEAKER_00]: We heard later on there were terrific storms all over Texas, mostly north and east of us.

1086
01:28:47,858 --> 01:28:52,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Flood warnings out in the valley, and tornadoes in various places.

1087
01:28:53,030 --> 01:29:07,417
[SPEAKER_00]: just apparently high winds, though, in the Odessa-Midland area, although they said the streets in Big Spring or Odessa, or not Odessa, but Pecos were covered with water, so it was all around us, apparently, the wind and the rain.

1088
01:29:07,617 --> 01:29:13,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But actually, it seemed quite nice in the motel room the last time we all had—we both had the best sleeps we've had, probably, in this trip.

1089
01:29:16,100 --> 01:29:21,423
[SPEAKER_00]: And all he brought out is your book for

1090
01:29:21,835 --> 01:29:22,976
[SPEAKER_00]: Mansfield.

1091
01:29:23,276 --> 01:29:27,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Mansfield must have been kind of a normal school, a high school.

1092
01:29:27,599 --> 01:29:30,321
[SPEAKER_00]: He went there three years to finish up his high school, then went to Lafayette.

1093
01:29:31,622 --> 01:29:36,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And he didn't have a yearbook for Lafayette.

1094
01:29:36,945 --> 01:29:39,707
[SPEAKER_00]: He had the one for Mansfield.

1095
01:29:39,747 --> 01:29:44,010
[SPEAKER_00]: He brought out a couple of folders full of pictures and clippings.

1096
01:29:44,691 --> 01:29:49,234
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't really get a chance to look at the clippings or the mementos very much.

1097
01:29:50,234 --> 01:29:50,755
[SPEAKER_00]: A couple of

1098
01:29:52,093 --> 01:29:53,934
[SPEAKER_00]: of banquets where he was honored.

1099
01:29:53,974 --> 01:30:05,282
[SPEAKER_00]: They had the program with the speakers and the menu and so on in the printed, folded menu there.

1100
01:30:08,664 --> 01:30:13,647
[SPEAKER_00]: One from Lafayette, one old-timers, a banquet he attended and was honored at.

1101
01:30:17,049 --> 01:30:18,650
[SPEAKER_00]: Pictures were not much.

1102
01:30:18,951 --> 01:30:21,052
[SPEAKER_00]: A couple of Hollywood stars.

1103
01:30:22,386 --> 01:30:26,829
[SPEAKER_00]: And I took copies, made copies with my 35-millimeter camera of most of them.

1104
01:30:27,729 --> 01:30:29,650
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't known if they'll come out or not, I don't know.

1105
01:30:30,331 --> 01:30:33,112
[SPEAKER_00]: I had enough light and the light meter looked like it was registering all right.

1106
01:30:34,153 --> 01:30:36,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, holding the camera steady is another matter.

1107
01:30:36,974 --> 01:30:39,336
[SPEAKER_00]: We'll have to see how those pictures came out.

1108
01:30:39,856 --> 01:30:44,359
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the dogs came in during the interview, or I guess when we were about through and I was looking at pictures.

1109
01:30:45,199 --> 01:30:46,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was actually very quiet then.

1110
01:30:46,940 --> 01:30:49,982
[SPEAKER_00]: He was friendly and didn't make a bit of noise.

1111
01:30:50,002 --> 01:30:51,503
[SPEAKER_00]: He just nestled down there.

1112
01:30:52,758 --> 01:30:55,699
[SPEAKER_00]: and watch proceedings from his vantage point on the floor.

1113
01:31:00,040 --> 01:31:10,002
[SPEAKER_00]: He took me out into his bedroom, went to the dining room, and then into his bedroom off the dining room, which he had some pictures on the wall there, a couple of little trophies.

1114
01:31:11,582 --> 01:31:13,263
[SPEAKER_00]: But he just didn't say much.

1115
01:31:14,023 --> 01:31:16,323
[SPEAKER_00]: He had just so much he could have said.

1116
01:31:17,424 --> 01:31:19,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And it seemed like a time he just

1117
01:31:21,023 --> 01:31:25,724
[SPEAKER_00]: didn't want to remember, or maybe he didn't think I'd be interested in his remembering.

1118
01:31:26,725 --> 01:31:38,028
[SPEAKER_00]: He just didn't elaborate the way I thought he would, because he was a bright enough chap, although he did have a stroke, as you mentioned, and probably that hurt, bothered his memory a little bit.

1119
01:31:39,229 --> 01:31:42,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And when he got going, though, he expressed himself well enough.

1120
01:31:44,730 --> 01:31:46,111
[SPEAKER_00]: I just don't know.

1121
01:31:46,191 --> 01:31:47,731
[SPEAKER_00]: It was not an easy interview.

1122
01:31:47,831 --> 01:31:50,212
[SPEAKER_00]: I was constantly having to prime him for

1123
01:31:50,855 --> 01:31:56,641
[SPEAKER_00]: more information and sometimes it couldn't respond much to my prayer.

1124
01:31:58,043 --> 01:31:59,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Stories about Ruth.

1125
01:31:59,344 --> 01:31:59,925
[SPEAKER_01]: What stories?

1126
01:32:00,005 --> 01:32:08,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh my God, there are all kinds of stories about Ruth and drinking and women and his carrying on and his spending of money and doing this.

